<?xml version="1.0" ?>
<!DOCTYPE PubmedArticleSet PUBLIC "-//NLM//DTD PubMedArticle, 1st January 2025//EN" "https://dtd.nlm.nih.gov/ncbi/pubmed/out/pubmed_250101.dtd">
<PubmedArticleSet>
<PubmedArticle><MedlineCitation Status="MEDLINE" Owner="NLM" IndexingMethod="Automated"><PMID Version="1">42916424</PMID><DateCompleted><Year>2026</Year><Month>10</Month><Day>23</Day></DateCompleted><DateRevised><Year>2026</Year><Month>10</Month><Day>23</Day></DateRevised><Article PubModel="Print"><Journal><ISSN IssnType="Electronic">1520-5851</ISSN><JournalIssue CitedMedium="Internet"><Volume>60</Volume><Issue>38</Issue><PubDate><Year>2026</Year><Month>Sep</Month><Day>29</Day></PubDate></JournalIssue><Title>Environmental science &amp; technology</Title><ISOAbbreviation>Environ Sci Technol</ISOAbbreviation></Journal><ArticleTitle>Drivers of Seasonal Variations of Greenhouse Gas Fluxes from Sediment-Water and Water-Atmosphere Interfaces in Different Fish Culture Ponds.</ArticleTitle><Pagination><StartPage>27215</StartPage><EndPage>27225</EndPage><MedlinePgn>27215-27225</MedlinePgn></Pagination><ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1021/acs.est.6c05696</ELocationID><Abstract><AbstractText>Aquaculture ponds constitute critical greenhouse gas (GHG) emission hotspots, yet limited research has concurrently quantified CH4, CO2, and N2O fluxes across sediment-water (SWI) and water-atmosphere (WAI) interfaces and their environmental and microbial controls under different aquaculture regimes. This study explored seasonal and diurnal GHG variations in four subtropical freshwater aquaculture ponds (Crucian carp, CruC; Largemouth bass, LarB; Channel catfish, ChaC; and in-pond raceway system of channel catfish, IPRS) in eastern China. SWI dominated CH4 diffusion, with an average flux of 139.1 &#xb1; 120.98 &#x3bc;mol&#xb7;m-2&#xb7;h-1, nearly 20 times the WAI CH4 flux. In contrast, WAI contributed 88.3% and 88% of total diffusive CO2 and N2O, respectively. CruC yielded the highest CH4 emissions (20267.06 &#x3bc;mol&#xb7;m-2&#xb7;h-1) and N2O emissions (8.73 &#x3bc;mol&#xb7;m-2&#xb7;h-1), while the IPRS exhibited the lowest, modulated primarily by temperature and sediment properties. Diurnally, FCH4 peaked in the daytime, whereas CO2 emissions were elevated at night, controlled by dissolved oxygen and pH. NMDS separated CH4 and N2O cycling microbial communities (mcrA, pmoA, nirS, nirK, nosZ) among pond types. PLS-PM verified sediment total nitrogen (STN) as the foremost positive driver of GHG fluxes across all interfaces. Our results confirm SWI predominance in diffusive CH4 production and demonstrate that interface partitioning is essential for refining regional aquatic carbon and nitrogen budgets.</AbstractText><CopyrightInformation>&#xa9; 2026 American Chemical Society.</CopyrightInformation></Abstract><AuthorList CompleteYN="Y"><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Liu</LastName><ForeName>Yuqi</ForeName><Initials>Y</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>College of Oceanography, Hohai University, Nanjing210024, China.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Zeng</LastName><ForeName>Qingfei</ForeName><Initials>Q</Initials><Identifier Source="ORCID">0000-0002-4608-6394</Identifier><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>State Key Laboratory of Lake and Watershed Science for Water Security, Nanjing Institute of Geography and Limnology, Chinese Academy of Sciences, Nanjing211135, China.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Fathulloevich</LastName><ForeName>Azizov Farukh</ForeName><Initials>AF</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Department of Ichthyology and Physiology of Agricultural Animals, Faculty of Zooengineering, Tajik Agrarian University Named After Shirinsho Shotemur, Rudaki ave. 146, Dushanbe734003, Republic of Tajikistan.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Zokirovna</LastName><ForeName>Teshaeva Madina</ForeName><Initials>TM</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Department of Ichthyology and Physiology of Agricultural Animals, Faculty of Zooengineering, Tajik Agrarian University Named After Shirinsho Shotemur, Rudaki ave. 146, Dushanbe734003, Republic of Tajikistan.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Gu</LastName><ForeName>Xiaohong</ForeName><Initials>X</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>State Key Laboratory of Lake and Watershed Science for Water Security, Nanjing Institute of Geography and Limnology, Chinese Academy of Sciences, Nanjing211135, China.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Yan</LastName><ForeName>Jie</ForeName><Initials>J</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>State Key Laboratory of Lake and Watershed Science for Water Security, Nanjing Institute of Geography and Limnology, Chinese Academy of Sciences, Nanjing211135, China.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>University of Chinese Academy of Sciences, Beijing100049, China.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Hong</LastName><ForeName>Li</ForeName><Initials>L</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>State Key Laboratory of Lake and Watershed Science for Water Security, Nanjing Institute of Geography and Limnology, Chinese Academy of Sciences, Nanjing211135, China.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>School of Environmental Science and Engineering, Nanjing University of Information Engineering, Nanjing210044, China.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Li</LastName><ForeName>Yifan</ForeName><Initials>Y</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>State Key Laboratory of Lake and Watershed Science for Water Security, Nanjing Institute of Geography and Limnology, Chinese Academy of Sciences, Nanjing211135, China.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>School of Environmental Science and Engineering, Nanjing University of Information Engineering, Nanjing210044, China.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Zhao</LastName><ForeName>Zhe</ForeName><Initials>Z</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>College of Oceanography, Hohai University, Nanjing210024, China.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author></AuthorList><Language>eng</Language><GrantList CompleteYN="Y"><Grant><GrantID>CX [23]1007</GrantID><Agency>Jiangsu Provincial Agricultural Science and Technology Independent Innovation Fund</Agency><Country/></Grant><Grant><GrantID>2025YFE0103700</GrantID><Agency>National Basic Research Program of China (973 Program)</Agency><Country/></Grant><Grant><GrantID>BE2022422</GrantID><Agency>Provincial science and technology innovative program for carbon peak and carbon neutrality of Jiangsu of China</Agency><Country/></Grant><Grant><GrantID>JCTG202529</GrantID><Agency>Jiangsu Modern Agriculture Key Technology Integration and Promotion Project</Agency><Country/></Grant></GrantList><PublicationTypeList><PublicationType UI="D016428">Journal Article</PublicationType></PublicationTypeList></Article><MedlineJournalInfo><Country>United States</Country><MedlineTA>Environ Sci Technol</MedlineTA><NlmUniqueID>0213155</NlmUniqueID><ISSNLinking>0013-936X</ISSNLinking></MedlineJournalInfo><ChemicalList><Chemical><RegistryNumber>0</RegistryNumber><NameOfSubstance UI="D000074382">Greenhouse Gases</NameOfSubstance></Chemical><Chemical><RegistryNumber>OP0UW79H66</RegistryNumber><NameOfSubstance UI="D008697">Methane</NameOfSubstance></Chemical><Chemical><RegistryNumber>142M471B3J</RegistryNumber><NameOfSubstance UI="D002245">Carbon Dioxide</NameOfSubstance></Chemical></ChemicalList><CitationSubset>IM</CitationSubset><MeshHeadingList><MeshHeading><DescriptorName UI="D000074382" MajorTopicYN="Y">Greenhouse Gases</DescriptorName></MeshHeading><MeshHeading><DescriptorName UI="D012621" MajorTopicYN="N">Seasons</DescriptorName></MeshHeading><MeshHeading><DescriptorName UI="D061005" MajorTopicYN="N">Ponds</DescriptorName></MeshHeading><MeshHeading><DescriptorName UI="D000818" MajorTopicYN="N">Animals</DescriptorName></MeshHeading><MeshHeading><DescriptorName UI="D017756" MajorTopicYN="N">Aquaculture</DescriptorName></MeshHeading><MeshHeading><DescriptorName UI="D019015" MajorTopicYN="N">Geologic Sediments</DescriptorName></MeshHeading><MeshHeading><DescriptorName UI="D001272" MajorTopicYN="N">Atmosphere</DescriptorName></MeshHeading><MeshHeading><DescriptorName UI="D008697" MajorTopicYN="N">Methane</DescriptorName></MeshHeading><MeshHeading><DescriptorName UI="D002681" MajorTopicYN="N" Type="Geographic">China</DescriptorName></MeshHeading><MeshHeading><DescriptorName UI="D002245" MajorTopicYN="N">Carbon Dioxide</DescriptorName></MeshHeading></MeshHeadingList><KeywordList Owner="NOTNLM"><Keyword MajorTopicYN="N">aquaculture ponds</Keyword><Keyword MajorTopicYN="N">carbon&#x2212;nitrogen cycling</Keyword><Keyword MajorTopicYN="N">functional microbes</Keyword><Keyword MajorTopicYN="N">greenhouse gases</Keyword><Keyword MajorTopicYN="N">sediment-water interface</Keyword><Keyword MajorTopicYN="N">water-atmosphere interface</Keyword></KeywordList></MedlineCitation><PubmedData><History><PubMedPubDate PubStatus="received"><Year>2026</Year><Month>4</Month><Day>16</Day></PubMedPubDate><PubMedPubDate PubStatus="accepted"><Year>2026</Year><Month>9</Month><Day>8</Day></PubMedPubDate><PubMedPubDate PubStatus="revised"><Year>2026</Year><Month>9</Month><Day>5</Day></PubMedPubDate><PubMedPubDate PubStatus="medline"><Year>2026</Year><Month>10</Month><Day>23</Day><Hour>23</Hour><Minute>32</Minute></PubMedPubDate><PubMedPubDate PubStatus="pubmed"><Year>2026</Year><Month>10</Month><Day>23</Day><Hour>23</Hour><Minute>31</Minute></PubMedPubDate><PubMedPubDate PubStatus="entrez"><Year>2026</Year><Month>10</Month><Day>23</Day><Hour>19</Hour><Minute>16</Minute></PubMedPubDate></History><PublicationStatus>ppublish</PublicationStatus><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">42916424</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.1021/acs.est.6c05696</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pii">5429759</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></PubmedData></PubmedArticle><PubmedArticle><MedlineCitation Status="Publisher" Owner="NLM"><PMID Version="1">42913813</PMID><DateRevised><Year>2026</Year><Month>10</Month><Day>23</Day></DateRevised><Article PubModel="Print-Electronic"><Journal><ISSN IssnType="Electronic">1873-2976</ISSN><JournalIssue CitedMedium="Internet"><PubDate><Year>2026</Year><Month>Oct</Month><Day>22</Day></PubDate></JournalIssue><Title>Bioresource technology</Title><ISOAbbreviation>Bioresour Technol</ISOAbbreviation></Journal><ArticleTitle>Intermittent illumination enables reversible carbon buffering by Rhodopseudomonas palustris for enhanced methane recovery from food waste anaerobic digestion.</ArticleTitle><Pagination><StartPage>136115</StartPage><MedlinePgn>136115</MedlinePgn></Pagination><ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.biortech.2026.136115</ELocationID><ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0960-8524(26)02197-8</ELocationID><Abstract><AbstractText>Anaerobic digestion (AD) of food waste (FW) is vulnerable to acidification when fermentation outpaces syntrophic oxidation and methanogenesis. To address this temporal imbalance, we introduced Rhodopseudomonas palustris (R. palustris), which can assimilate fermentation-derived volatile fatty acids (VFAs) and store carbon as polyhydroxyalkanoates (PHAs). Intermittent illumination reduced VFA accumulation and end-cycle H<sub>2</sub> partial pressure, while yielding the highest mean daily methane production (589.0&#x202f;mL d<sup>-1</sup>) and fraction of carbon recovered as methane (37.2&#x202f;%). A pure-culture assay showed that PHA accumulated during illumination and declined in darkness, indicating reversible intracellular carbon storage and remobilization. The PHA turnover pattern paralleled reactor methane dynamics, which was consistent with a temporal association between carbon storage and downstream methanogenesis. Intermittent illumination yielded the lowest NADH/NAD<sup>+</sup> ratio while maintaining high ATP availability. Community analysis revealed a more balanced distribution of functional guilds between the liquid and reactor-wall fractions, while metagenomic analysis indicated functional potential for PHA turnover and methanogenesis. Together, these findings support a carbon-buffering model in which light-dark cycling is associated with transient carbon storage and subsequent remobilization, potentially improving the temporal coupling between acidogenesis and methane formation and enhancing AD stability and methane recovery.</AbstractText><CopyrightInformation>Copyright &#xa9; 2026. Published by Elsevier Ltd.</CopyrightInformation></Abstract><AuthorList CompleteYN="Y"><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Zhang</LastName><ForeName>Wenjing</ForeName><Initials>W</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Key Laboratory of the Three Gorges Reservoir Region's Eco-environments, Ministry of Education, Institute of Environment and Ecology, Chongqing University, 174 Shapingba Road, Chongqing 400045, China.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Li</LastName><ForeName>Xiaoyun</ForeName><Initials>X</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Key Laboratory of the Three Gorges Reservoir Region's Eco-environments, Ministry of Education, Institute of Environment and Ecology, Chongqing University, 174 Shapingba Road, Chongqing 400045, China.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Zhang</LastName><ForeName>Xinyu</ForeName><Initials>X</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Key Laboratory of the Three Gorges Reservoir Region's Eco-environments, Ministry of Education, Institute of Environment and Ecology, Chongqing University, 174 Shapingba Road, Chongqing 400045, China.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Zhang</LastName><ForeName>Wanquan</ForeName><Initials>W</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Key Laboratory of the Three Gorges Reservoir Region's Eco-environments, Ministry of Education, Institute of Environment and Ecology, Chongqing University, 174 Shapingba Road, Chongqing 400045, China.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Gu</LastName><ForeName>Li</ForeName><Initials>L</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Key Laboratory of the Three Gorges Reservoir Region's Eco-environments, Ministry of Education, Institute of Environment and Ecology, Chongqing University, 174 Shapingba Road, Chongqing 400045, China. Electronic address: guli@cqu.edu.cn.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Chen</LastName><ForeName>Yongdong</ForeName><Initials>Y</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>College of Chemistry and Chemical Engineering, China University of Petroleum (East China), Qingdao 266580, China. Electronic address: chenyongdong@upc.edu.cn.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Ai</LastName><ForeName>Hainan</ForeName><Initials>H</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Key Laboratory of the Three Gorges Reservoir Region's Eco-environments, Ministry of Education, Institute of Environment and Ecology, Chongqing University, 174 Shapingba Road, Chongqing 400045, China.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author></AuthorList><Language>eng</Language><PublicationTypeList><PublicationType UI="D016428">Journal Article</PublicationType></PublicationTypeList><ArticleDate DateType="Electronic"><Year>2026</Year><Month>10</Month><Day>22</Day></ArticleDate></Article><MedlineJournalInfo><Country>England</Country><MedlineTA>Bioresour Technol</MedlineTA><NlmUniqueID>9889523</NlmUniqueID><ISSNLinking>0960-8524</ISSNLinking></MedlineJournalInfo><CitationSubset>IM</CitationSubset><KeywordList Owner="NOTNLM"><Keyword MajorTopicYN="N">Anaerobic digestion</Keyword><Keyword MajorTopicYN="N">Food waste</Keyword><Keyword MajorTopicYN="N">Intermittent illumination</Keyword><Keyword MajorTopicYN="N">Methane recovery</Keyword><Keyword MajorTopicYN="N">Polyhydroxyalkanoate turnover</Keyword><Keyword MajorTopicYN="N">Rhodopseudomonas palustris</Keyword></KeywordList><CoiStatement>Declaration of competing interest The authors declare that they have no known competing financial interests or personal relationships that could have appeared to influence the work reported in this paper.</CoiStatement></MedlineCitation><PubmedData><History><PubMedPubDate PubStatus="received"><Year>2026</Year><Month>8</Month><Day>31</Day></PubMedPubDate><PubMedPubDate PubStatus="revised"><Year>2026</Year><Month>9</Month><Day>30</Day></PubMedPubDate><PubMedPubDate PubStatus="accepted"><Year>2026</Year><Month>10</Month><Day>21</Day></PubMedPubDate><PubMedPubDate PubStatus="medline"><Year>2026</Year><Month>10</Month><Day>23</Day><Hour>23</Hour><Minute>31</Minute></PubMedPubDate><PubMedPubDate PubStatus="pubmed"><Year>2026</Year><Month>10</Month><Day>23</Day><Hour>23</Hour><Minute>31</Minute></PubMedPubDate><PubMedPubDate PubStatus="entrez"><Year>2026</Year><Month>10</Month><Day>23</Day><Hour>18</Hour><Minute>0</Minute></PubMedPubDate></History><PublicationStatus>aheadofprint</PublicationStatus><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">42913813</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.1016/j.biortech.2026.136115</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pii">S0960-8524(26)02197-8</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></PubmedData></PubmedArticle><PubmedArticle><MedlineCitation Status="Publisher" Owner="NLM"><PMID Version="1">42906136</PMID><DateRevised><Year>2026</Year><Month>10</Month><Day>22</Day></DateRevised><Article PubModel="Print-Electronic"><Journal><ISSN IssnType="Electronic">1537-1719</ISSN><JournalIssue CitedMedium="Internet"><PubDate><Year>2026</Year><Month>Oct</Month><Day>21</Day></PubDate></JournalIssue><Title>Molecular biology and evolution</Title><ISOAbbreviation>Mol Biol Evol</ISOAbbreviation></Journal><ArticleTitle>Early holobiont-level divergence and adaptation across seafloor geochemical contrasts in the deep-sea squat lobster Shinkaia crosnieri.</ArticleTitle><ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">msag262</ELocationID><ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1093/molbev/msag262</ELocationID><Abstract><AbstractText>Deep-sea hydrothermal vents and methane seeps host unique animal communities sustained by chemosynthetic symbioses, yet how biological diversification proceeds across these geochemically distinct and geographically fragmented habitats remains poorly understood. Previous studies have largely focused on either host genomic adaptation or symbiont diversity as drivers of isolation, leaving unresolved how environmental geochemistry and host-microbe associations jointly shape evolutionary trajectories in chemosynthetic ecosystems. Here, we addressed this gap by combining chromosome-level host genomics, population resequencing, and episymbiont metagenomics in the deep-sea chemosynthetic lobster Shinkaia crosnieri, a dominant species inhabiting both hydrothermal vents and methane seeps. Genomic innovations-including sensory trade-offs (vision loss versus chemosensory expansion), metabolic compensation for pigment loss, and positive selection on respiratory protein-underpin deep-sea colonization of S. crosnieri. Populations from the Okinawa Trough vent and the South China Sea seep exhibit pronounced genetic divergence, potentially driven by Pleistocene vicariance and reinforced by divergent selection across sulfur-enriched (vent) versus sulfur-depleted, methane-dominated (seep) environments. This selection target host genes involved in chemoreception and immunity, with coordinated signatures in both sequence evolution and structural variation. Crucially, holobiont-level adaptation is amplified by habitat-specific recruitment of episymbionts: sulfur-oxidizing Campylobacterales/Thiotrichales dominate vent-associated lobsters, while methanotrophic Methylococcales prevail at seep counterparts. These symbionts partition energy niches metabolically, with seep-associated communities enhancing biosynthesis of key cofactors (e.g., coenzyme Q, tetrahydrofolate). Host transporters for these nutrients are concurrently upregulated, demonstrating functional synergy. Our findings establish that diversification in extreme environments operates holobiont-wide, where geochemical gradients synergistically shape host genomic plasticity and symbiont metabolic specialization to drive early-stage divergence.</AbstractText><CopyrightInformation>&#xa9; The Author(s) 2026. Published by Oxford University Press on behalf of Society for Molecular Biology and Evolution.</CopyrightInformation></Abstract><AuthorList CompleteYN="Y"><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Cheng</LastName><ForeName>Jiao</ForeName><Initials>J</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Laboratory of Marine Organism Taxonomy and Phylogeny, Shandong Province Key Laboratory of Marine Biodiversity and Bio-resource Sustainable Utilization, Institute of Oceanology, Chinese Academy of Sciences, Qingdao 266071, China.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Laboratory for Marine Biology and Biotechnology, Qingdao Marine Science and Technology Center, Qingdao 266237, China.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Hui</LastName><ForeName>Min</ForeName><Initials>M</Initials><Identifier Source="ORCID">0000-0002-6901-6524</Identifier><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Laboratory of Marine Organism Taxonomy and Phylogeny, Shandong Province Key Laboratory of Marine Biodiversity and Bio-resource Sustainable Utilization, Institute of Oceanology, Chinese Academy of Sciences, Qingdao 266071, China.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Laboratory for Marine Biology and Biotechnology, Qingdao Marine Science and Technology Center, Qingdao 266237, China.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Yu</LastName><ForeName>Mengjun</ForeName><Initials>M</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>BGI Research Institute, Qingdao 266555, China.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Wang</LastName><ForeName>Minxiao</ForeName><Initials>M</Initials><Identifier Source="ORCID">0000-0002-6567-3295</Identifier><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Center of Deep Sea Research, Qingdao Key Laboratory of Intelligent Exploitation of Deep-sea Bioresources, Institute of Oceanology, Chinese Academy of Sciences, Qingdao 266000, China.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Sha</LastName><ForeName>Zhongli</ForeName><Initials>Z</Initials><Identifier Source="ORCID">0000-0002-2192-3758</Identifier><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Laboratory of Marine Organism Taxonomy and Phylogeny, Shandong Province Key Laboratory of Marine Biodiversity and Bio-resource Sustainable Utilization, Institute of Oceanology, Chinese Academy of Sciences, Qingdao 266071, China.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Laboratory for Marine Biology and Biotechnology, Qingdao Marine Science and Technology Center, Qingdao 266237, China.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author></AuthorList><Language>eng</Language><PublicationTypeList><PublicationType UI="D016428">Journal Article</PublicationType></PublicationTypeList><ArticleDate DateType="Electronic"><Year>2026</Year><Month>10</Month><Day>21</Day></ArticleDate></Article><MedlineJournalInfo><Country>United States</Country><MedlineTA>Mol Biol Evol</MedlineTA><NlmUniqueID>8501455</NlmUniqueID><ISSNLinking>0737-4038</ISSNLinking></MedlineJournalInfo><CitationSubset>IM</CitationSubset><KeywordList Owner="NOTNLM"><Keyword MajorTopicYN="N">deep-sea chemosynthetic ecosystems</Keyword><Keyword MajorTopicYN="N">episymbiosis</Keyword><Keyword MajorTopicYN="N">habitat-linked divergence</Keyword><Keyword MajorTopicYN="N">metagenomics</Keyword><Keyword MajorTopicYN="N">population genomics</Keyword></KeywordList></MedlineCitation><PubmedData><History><PubMedPubDate PubStatus="received"><Year>2026</Year><Month>6</Month><Day>22</Day></PubMedPubDate><PubMedPubDate PubStatus="revised"><Year>2026</Year><Month>9</Month><Day>2</Day></PubMedPubDate><PubMedPubDate PubStatus="medline"><Year>2026</Year><Month>10</Month><Day>22</Day><Hour>23</Hour><Minute>29</Minute></PubMedPubDate><PubMedPubDate PubStatus="pubmed"><Year>2026</Year><Month>10</Month><Day>22</Day><Hour>23</Hour><Minute>29</Minute></PubMedPubDate><PubMedPubDate PubStatus="entrez"><Year>2026</Year><Month>10</Month><Day>22</Day><Hour>19</Hour><Minute>20</Minute></PubMedPubDate></History><PublicationStatus>aheadofprint</PublicationStatus><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">42906136</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.1093/molbev/msag262</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pii">8965425</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></PubmedData></PubmedArticle><PubmedArticle><MedlineCitation Status="Publisher" Owner="NLM"><PMID Version="1">42904908</PMID><DateRevised><Year>2026</Year><Month>10</Month><Day>22</Day></DateRevised><Article PubModel="Print-Electronic"><Journal><ISSN IssnType="Electronic">1479-487X</ISSN><JournalIssue CitedMedium="Internet"><PubDate><Year>2026</Year><Month>Oct</Month><Day>21</Day></PubDate></JournalIssue><Title>Environmental technology</Title><ISOAbbreviation>Environ Technol</ISOAbbreviation></Journal><ArticleTitle>ZVI combined with alkaline pretreatment optimizes FW and RS anaerobic co-digestion: methanogenic potential, acidification mitigation, and microbial community regulation.</ArticleTitle><Pagination><StartPage>1</StartPage><EndPage>15</EndPage><MedlinePgn>1-15</MedlinePgn></Pagination><ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1080/09593330.2026.2749493</ELocationID><Abstract><AbstractText>Food waste and residual sludge co-digestion suffers from poor biodegradability and frequent acid inhibition, due to abundant refractory organics and dense extracellular polymers. Batch anaerobic digestion experiments were performed to explore the combined promotion effect of alkaline pretreatment combined with zero-valent iron (ZVI) on the co-digestion of food waste and residual sludge. Preliminary trials confirmed that 3% NaOH was optimal for substrate disintegration. Under the tested conditions, the numerically highest cumulative methane yield was achieved at the F4 group (5 g/L ZVI), which was 62.72% and 19.61% higher than the untreated KB and alkali-only F0 groups, respectively. Moderate ZVI improved system pH buffering, lowered oxidation-reduction potential (ORP), accelerated the conversion of acetic and propionic acids, and reduced acidification risk. Fe<sup>2+</sup> presented a three-stage variation governed by ZVI corrosion, chemical precipitation and microbial assimilation. High-throughput sequencing revealed ZVI enriched hydrolytic Bacteroidetes and mixotrophic <i>Methanosarcina</i> while decreasing the proportion of hydrogenotrophic <i>Methanobacterium</i>. Redundancy analysis (RDA) supported indirectly Fe<sup>2+</sup>, ORP and pH correlated with microbial succession and methanogenic performance. Excessive ZVI triggered performance decline, which may arise from potential salting-out effects, further experimental validation is required. This study clarifies the coupling mechanism of alkali-ZVI and provides technical guidance for organic solid waste resource recovery.</AbstractText></Abstract><AuthorList CompleteYN="Y"><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Xue</LastName><ForeName>Tongzhan</ForeName><Initials>T</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>School of Environment and Energy Engineering, Anhui Jianzhu University, Hefei, People's Republic of China.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Anhui Provincial Department of Science and Technology, Anhui Provincial Key Laboratory of Environmental Pollution Control and Resource Reuse, Hefei, People's Republic of China.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Chen</LastName><ForeName>Lidong</ForeName><Initials>L</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>School of Environment and Energy Engineering, Anhui Jianzhu University, Hefei, People's Republic of China.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Yan</LastName><ForeName>Xiangyu</ForeName><Initials>X</Initials><Identifier Source="ORCID">0009-0009-8075-2459</Identifier><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>School of Environment and Energy Engineering, Anhui Jianzhu University, Hefei, People's Republic of China.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Li</LastName><ForeName>Weihua</ForeName><Initials>W</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>School of Environment and Energy Engineering, Anhui Jianzhu University, Hefei, People's Republic of China.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Anhui Provincial Department of Science and Technology, Anhui Provincial Key Laboratory of Environmental Pollution Control and Resource Reuse, Hefei, People's Republic of China.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Yang</LastName><ForeName>Houyun</ForeName><Initials>H</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>School of Environment and Energy Engineering, Anhui Jianzhu University, Hefei, People's Republic of China.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Anhui Provincial Department of Science and Technology, Anhui Provincial Key Laboratory of Environmental Pollution Control and Resource Reuse, Hefei, People's Republic of China.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Yu</LastName><ForeName>Li</ForeName><Initials>L</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>School of Environment and Energy Engineering, Anhui Jianzhu University, Hefei, People's Republic of China.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Chen</LastName><ForeName>Xinyu</ForeName><Initials>X</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>School of Environment and Energy Engineering, Anhui Jianzhu University, Hefei, People's Republic of China.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Dong</LastName><ForeName>Bo</ForeName><Initials>B</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>School of Environment and Energy Engineering, Anhui Jianzhu University, Hefei, People's Republic of China.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author></AuthorList><Language>eng</Language><PublicationTypeList><PublicationType UI="D016428">Journal Article</PublicationType></PublicationTypeList><ArticleDate DateType="Electronic"><Year>2026</Year><Month>10</Month><Day>21</Day></ArticleDate></Article><MedlineJournalInfo><Country>England</Country><MedlineTA>Environ Technol</MedlineTA><NlmUniqueID>9884939</NlmUniqueID><ISSNLinking>0959-3330</ISSNLinking></MedlineJournalInfo><CitationSubset>IM</CitationSubset><OtherAbstract Type="plain-language-summary" Language="eng"><AbstractText>The alkaline pretreatment effectively disrupted the dense structure of the refractory substances in food waste and residual sludge, promoting the release of soluble organic matters.Optimal 5 g/L ZVI coupled with alkaline pretreatment boosted cumulative methane by 62.72% relative to raw substrate, and excessive ZVI may be triggered salt-out inhibition of key functional enzymes.ZVI effectively stabilized digestion microenvironment via declining ORP, elevating pH buffer capacity and accelerating the degradation of refractory propionic and butyric acid.ZVI shifted dominant methanogen from single hydrogenotrophic <i>Methanobacterium</i> to mixotrophic <i>Methanosarcina</i>, diversifying methanogenic pathways.RDA quantitatively unveiled intrinsic linkage among Fe<sup>2+</sup> dynamics, environmental variables, microbial succession and final methanogenic efficiency.</AbstractText></OtherAbstract><KeywordList Owner="NOTNLM"><Keyword MajorTopicYN="N">Anaerobic digestion</Keyword><Keyword MajorTopicYN="N">alkaline pretreatment</Keyword><Keyword MajorTopicYN="N">high-throughput sequencing</Keyword><Keyword MajorTopicYN="N">microbial community</Keyword><Keyword MajorTopicYN="N">oxidation&#x2013;reduction potential</Keyword></KeywordList></MedlineCitation><PubmedData><History><PubMedPubDate PubStatus="medline"><Year>2026</Year><Month>10</Month><Day>22</Day><Hour>17</Hour><Minute>33</Minute></PubMedPubDate><PubMedPubDate PubStatus="pubmed"><Year>2026</Year><Month>10</Month><Day>22</Day><Hour>17</Hour><Minute>33</Minute></PubMedPubDate><PubMedPubDate PubStatus="entrez"><Year>2026</Year><Month>10</Month><Day>22</Day><Hour>16</Hour><Minute>54</Minute></PubMedPubDate></History><PublicationStatus>aheadofprint</PublicationStatus><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">42904908</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.1080/09593330.2026.2749493</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></PubmedData></PubmedArticle><PubmedArticle><MedlineCitation Status="Publisher" Owner="NLM"><PMID Version="1">42901359</PMID><DateRevised><Year>2026</Year><Month>10</Month><Day>20</Day></DateRevised><Article PubModel="Print-Electronic"><Journal><ISSN IssnType="Electronic">1873-2976</ISSN><JournalIssue CitedMedium="Internet"><PubDate><Year>2026</Year><Month>Oct</Month><Day>20</Day></PubDate></JournalIssue><Title>Bioresource technology</Title><ISOAbbreviation>Bioresour Technol</ISOAbbreviation></Journal><ArticleTitle>Novel strategy for advanced treatment of municipal wastewater with low carbon footprint: biological nitrogen removal and potent greenhouse gas mitigation in anaerobic-anoxic biofilter reactor by introducing raw wastewater.</ArticleTitle><Pagination><StartPage>136109</StartPage><MedlinePgn>136109</MedlinePgn></Pagination><ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.biortech.2026.136109</ELocationID><ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0960-8524(26)02191-7</ELocationID><Abstract><AbstractText>To address the high carbon footprint of wastewater treatment plants, this study developed an anaerobic-anoxic biofilter reactor (A<sup>2</sup>-BFR) with a large ratio of height to diameter through two-point inlet. Raw wastewater was fed at bottom inlet to produce methane anaerobically. Meanwhile, the secondary effluent containing NO<sub>3</sub><sup>-</sup>-N was fed at middle inlet to produce NO<sub>2</sub><sup>-</sup>-N, which could be further removed through nitrite-dependent anaerobic methane oxidation (n-DAMO) and anaerobic ammonium oxidation (AMX). The results demonstrated that the effluent concentrations of TN and NH<sub>4</sub><sup>+</sup>-N in A<sup>2</sup>-BFR remained 12.22 and 1.95&#x202f;mg&#x202f;N/L, when the flow ratio of raw wastewater to secondary effluent was 1: 5. The apparent dissolved CH<sub>4</sub> attenuation rate was approximately 7.60&#x202f;g CH<sub>4</sub>/(m<sup>3</sup>&#xb7;d), leading the effluent dissolved CH<sub>4</sub> concentration remained around 0.98&#x202f;mg/L. Microbial community analysis revealed that DAMO bacteria (Candidatus Methylomirabilis) and anammox bacteria (Candidatus Brocadia) acted as the core consortium for nitrogen removal, jointly responsible for NO<sub>2</sub><sup>-</sup>-N removal. Typical denitrifier bacteria Denitratisoma and sulfur autotrophic denitrifying bacteria Thiobacillus could reduce NO<sub>3</sub><sup>-</sup>-N to NO<sub>2</sub><sup>-</sup>-N, and ammonia oxidizing archaea such as Candidatus Nitrosocosmicus and norank_f_Nitrososphaeraceae could also oxidize NH<sub>4</sub><sup>+</sup>-N to NO<sub>2</sub><sup>-</sup>-N, which stably supplied substrates for the core microbes of nitrogen removal and promoted their enrichment and functional expression. Without external aeration and additional carbon sources, only feeding 16.67% raw wastewater to A<sup>2</sup>-BFR could ensure the secondary effluent of higher nitrate discharge in compliance with standards. This study provides a novel strategy for the advanced treatment of municipal wastewater with low carbon footprint.</AbstractText><CopyrightInformation>Copyright &#xa9; 2026. Published by Elsevier Ltd.</CopyrightInformation></Abstract><AuthorList CompleteYN="Y"><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Sun</LastName><ForeName>Meng-Yuan</ForeName><Initials>MY</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>School of Environmental and Municipal Engineering, Xi'an University of Architecture and Technology, China; Key Laboratory of Northwest Water Resource, Environment and Ecology, MOE, Xi'an University of Architecture and Technology, Xi'an 710055, China. Electronic address: 1547978397@qq.com.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Wang</LastName><ForeName>Yi</ForeName><Initials>Y</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>School of Environmental and Municipal Engineering, Xi'an University of Architecture and Technology, China; Key Laboratory of Northwest Water Resource, Environment and Ecology, MOE, Xi'an University of Architecture and Technology, Xi'an 710055, China. Electronic address: wangyi1003@xauat.edu.cn.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Wang</LastName><ForeName>Yue-Ning</ForeName><Initials>YN</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>School of Environmental and Municipal Engineering, Xi'an University of Architecture and Technology, China; Key Laboratory of Northwest Water Resource, Environment and Ecology, MOE, Xi'an University of Architecture and Technology, Xi'an 710055, China. Electronic address: 1009409891@qq.com.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Zhao</LastName><ForeName>Meng-Nan</ForeName><Initials>MN</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>School of Environmental and Municipal Engineering, Xi'an University of Architecture and Technology, China; Key Laboratory of Northwest Water Resource, Environment and Ecology, MOE, Xi'an University of Architecture and Technology, Xi'an 710055, China. Electronic address: 958448752@qq.com.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Liu</LastName><ForeName>Yong-Qiang</ForeName><Initials>YQ</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Faculty of Engineering and Physical Sciences, University of Southampton, Southampton SO17 1BJ, United Kingdom. Electronic address: y.liu@soton.ac.uk.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Zhang</LastName><ForeName>Xin</ForeName><Initials>X</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>School of Environmental and Municipal Engineering, Xi'an University of Architecture and Technology, China; Key Laboratory of Northwest Water Resource, Environment and Ecology, MOE, Xi'an University of Architecture and Technology, Xi'an 710055, China. Electronic address: 2583656362@qq.com.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Li</LastName><ForeName>Jie</ForeName><Initials>J</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>School of Environmental and Municipal Engineering, Xi'an University of Architecture and Technology, China; Key Laboratory of Northwest Water Resource, Environment and Ecology, MOE, Xi'an University of Architecture and Technology, Xi'an 710055, China. Electronic address: 2696198677@qq.com.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Wang</LastName><ForeName>Wen-Bo</ForeName><Initials>WB</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>School of Environmental and Municipal Engineering, Xi'an University of Architecture and Technology, China; Key Laboratory of Northwest Water Resource, Environment and Ecology, MOE, Xi'an University of Architecture and Technology, Xi'an 710055, China. Electronic address: 3347940032@qq.com.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author></AuthorList><Language>eng</Language><PublicationTypeList><PublicationType UI="D016428">Journal Article</PublicationType></PublicationTypeList><ArticleDate DateType="Electronic"><Year>2026</Year><Month>10</Month><Day>20</Day></ArticleDate></Article><MedlineJournalInfo><Country>England</Country><MedlineTA>Bioresour Technol</MedlineTA><NlmUniqueID>9889523</NlmUniqueID><ISSNLinking>0960-8524</ISSNLinking></MedlineJournalInfo><CitationSubset>IM</CitationSubset><KeywordList Owner="NOTNLM"><Keyword MajorTopicYN="N">A(2)-BFR</Keyword><Keyword MajorTopicYN="N">Nitrite-dependent anaerobic methane oxidation (n-DAMO)</Keyword><Keyword MajorTopicYN="N">Optimal flow ratio</Keyword><Keyword MajorTopicYN="N">Production and consumption of methane</Keyword><Keyword MajorTopicYN="N">Tertiary wastewater treatment</Keyword></KeywordList><CoiStatement>Declaration of competing interest The authors declare that they have no known competing financial interests or personal relationships that could have appeared to influence the work reported in this paper.</CoiStatement></MedlineCitation><PubmedData><History><PubMedPubDate PubStatus="received"><Year>2026</Year><Month>8</Month><Day>20</Day></PubMedPubDate><PubMedPubDate PubStatus="revised"><Year>2026</Year><Month>10</Month><Day>8</Day></PubMedPubDate><PubMedPubDate PubStatus="accepted"><Year>2026</Year><Month>10</Month><Day>20</Day></PubMedPubDate><PubMedPubDate PubStatus="medline"><Year>2026</Year><Month>10</Month><Day>20</Day><Hour>23</Hour><Minute>29</Minute></PubMedPubDate><PubMedPubDate PubStatus="pubmed"><Year>2026</Year><Month>10</Month><Day>20</Day><Hour>23</Hour><Minute>29</Minute></PubMedPubDate><PubMedPubDate PubStatus="entrez"><Year>2026</Year><Month>10</Month><Day>20</Day><Hour>19</Hour><Minute>37</Minute></PubMedPubDate></History><PublicationStatus>aheadofprint</PublicationStatus><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">42901359</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.1016/j.biortech.2026.136109</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pii">S0960-8524(26)02191-7</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></PubmedData></PubmedArticle><PubmedArticle><MedlineCitation Status="PubMed-not-MEDLINE" Owner="NLM"><PMID Version="1">42897614</PMID><DateCompleted><Year>2026</Year><Month>10</Month><Day>20</Day></DateCompleted><DateRevised><Year>2026</Year><Month>10</Month><Day>22</Day></DateRevised><Article PubModel="Electronic"><Journal><ISSN IssnType="Electronic">2524-6372</ISSN><JournalIssue CitedMedium="Internet"><Volume>21</Volume><Issue>1</Issue><PubDate><Year>2026</Year><Month>Jul</Month><Day>22</Day></PubDate></JournalIssue><Title>Environmental microbiome</Title><ISOAbbreviation>Environ Microbiome</ISOAbbreviation></Journal><ArticleTitle>Metagenomics identifies distinct functional and taxonomic profiles between freshwaters and associated sediments in alpine habitats.</ArticleTitle><ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">125</ELocationID><ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1186/s40793-026-00931-6</ELocationID><Abstract><AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Alpine freshwater habitats are biodiversity hotspots, home to uniquely adapted microbial communities. However, our understanding of how taxonomic composition and metabolic potential of microbial communities varies across habitat type and physiography remains limited.</AbstractText><AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Using metagenomic sequencing, we examined microbial diversity and functions in sediment and water habitats across a physiographic gradient, including deep and shallow lakes, pasture ponds, ponds and peat bogs. Overall, sediments supported more diverse and even microbial communities. Conversely, waters displayed higher heterogeneity across the physiographic gradient in both taxonomic and functional composition. Notably, water communities from small water bodies, especially peat bogs and ponds, showed comparable diversity to their respective sediment communities, with higher taxonomic richness than aquatic communities from larger water bodies. Sediment microbial communities showed higher functional potential for nitrogen and methane metabolism, a pattern related to the likely anoxic conditions. By contrast, water bodies favored higher potential for carbohydrate metabolism, carbon oxidation and photosynthesis. Lastly, we recovered 496 bacterial and archaeal metagenome-assembled genomes and found that 73% could not be assigned at the species level.</AbstractText><AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Our findings shed new light on the microbial diversity and metabolic potential of alpine freshwater ecosystems, highlighting the vast and unknown diversity harbored by these fragile alpine ecosystems.</AbstractText><CopyrightInformation>&#xa9; 2026. The Author(s).</CopyrightInformation></Abstract><AuthorList CompleteYN="Y"><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Gattei</LastName><ForeName>Anita</ForeName><Initials>A</Initials><Identifier Source="ORCID">0000-0002-1118-4461</Identifier><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Computational Biology Unit, Research and Innovation Centre, Fondazione Edmund Mach, via Mach 1, 38098, San Michele all'Adige, Italy.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>National Biodiversity Future Center (NBFC), Piazza Marina 61, 90133, Palermo, Italy.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Vettorazzo</LastName><ForeName>Sara</ForeName><Initials>S</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Hydrobiology Unit, Research and Innovation Centre, Fondazione Edmund Mach, via Mach 1, 38098, San Michele all'Adige, Italy.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>National Biodiversity Future Center (NBFC), Piazza Marina 61, 90133, Palermo, Italy.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Pindo</LastName><ForeName>Massimo</ForeName><Initials>M</Initials><Identifier Source="ORCID">0000-0002-8516-9010</Identifier><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Computational Biology Unit, Research and Innovation Centre, Fondazione Edmund Mach, via Mach 1, 38098, San Michele all'Adige, Italy.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Salmaso</LastName><ForeName>Nico</ForeName><Initials>N</Initials><Identifier Source="ORCID">0000-0001-6057-3642</Identifier><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Hydrobiology Unit, Research and Innovation Centre, Fondazione Edmund Mach, via Mach 1, 38098, San Michele all'Adige, Italy.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>National Biodiversity Future Center (NBFC), Piazza Marina 61, 90133, Palermo, Italy.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Manghi</LastName><ForeName>Paolo</ForeName><Initials>P</Initials><Identifier Source="ORCID">0000-0003-0846-6529</Identifier><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Computational Biology Unit, Research and Innovation Centre, Fondazione Edmund Mach, via Mach 1, 38098, San Michele all'Adige, Italy.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Larsen</LastName><ForeName>Stefano</ForeName><Initials>S</Initials><Identifier Source="ORCID">0000-0002-6774-1407</Identifier><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Computational Biology Unit, Research and Innovation Centre, Fondazione Edmund Mach, via Mach 1, 38098, San Michele all'Adige, Italy.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>National Biodiversity Future Center (NBFC), Piazza Marina 61, 90133, Palermo, Italy.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Donati</LastName><ForeName>Claudio</ForeName><Initials>C</Initials><Identifier Source="ORCID">0000-0001-8688-1651</Identifier><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Computational Biology Unit, Research and Innovation Centre, Fondazione Edmund Mach, via Mach 1, 38098, San Michele all'Adige, Italy. claudio.donati@fmach.it.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>National Biodiversity Future Center (NBFC), Piazza Marina 61, 90133, Palermo, Italy. claudio.donati@fmach.it.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author></AuthorList><Language>eng</Language><GrantList CompleteYN="Y"><Grant><GrantID>PNRR, Mission 4 Component 2, Investment 1.4, D.D. 1034 17/06/2022, Project CN00000033</GrantID><Agency>Ministero dell'Istruzione, dell'Universit&#xe0; e della Ricerca</Agency><Country/></Grant></GrantList><PublicationTypeList><PublicationType UI="D016428">Journal Article</PublicationType></PublicationTypeList><ArticleDate DateType="Electronic"><Year>2026</Year><Month>07</Month><Day>22</Day></ArticleDate></Article><MedlineJournalInfo><Country>England</Country><MedlineTA>Environ Microbiome</MedlineTA><NlmUniqueID>101768168</NlmUniqueID><ISSNLinking>2524-6372</ISSNLinking></MedlineJournalInfo><KeywordList Owner="NOTNLM"><Keyword MajorTopicYN="N">Biodiversity monitoring</Keyword><Keyword MajorTopicYN="N">Freshwater microbial ecology</Keyword><Keyword MajorTopicYN="N">Functional analysis</Keyword><Keyword MajorTopicYN="N">Metagenome-assembled genomes</Keyword><Keyword MajorTopicYN="N">Metagenomics</Keyword></KeywordList><CoiStatement>Declarations. Ethics approval and consent to participate: Not applicable Consent for publication: Not applicable Competing interest: The authors declare that they have no competing interest.</CoiStatement></MedlineCitation><PubmedData><History><PubMedPubDate PubStatus="received"><Year>2025</Year><Month>6</Month><Day>4</Day></PubMedPubDate><PubMedPubDate PubStatus="accepted"><Year>2026</Year><Month>7</Month><Day>1</Day></PubMedPubDate><PubMedPubDate PubStatus="medline"><Year>2026</Year><Month>10</Month><Day>20</Day><Hour>5</Hour><Minute>31</Minute></PubMedPubDate><PubMedPubDate PubStatus="pubmed"><Year>2026</Year><Month>10</Month><Day>20</Day><Hour>5</Hour><Minute>30</Minute></PubMedPubDate><PubMedPubDate PubStatus="entrez"><Year>2026</Year><Month>10</Month><Day>20</Day><Hour>0</Hour><Minute>59</Minute></PubMedPubDate><PubMedPubDate PubStatus="pmc-release"><Year>2026</Year><Month>7</Month><Day>22</Day></PubMedPubDate></History><PublicationStatus>epublish</PublicationStatus><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">42897614</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pmc">PMC13679186</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.1186/s40793-026-00931-6</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pii">10.1186/s40793-026-00931-6</ArticleId></ArticleIdList><ReferenceList><Reference><Citation>Delgado-Baquerizo M, Maestre FT, Reich PB, et al. Microbial diversity drives multifunctionality in terrestrial ecosystems. Nat Commun. 2016;7:10541. 10.1038/ncomms10541.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.1038/ncomms10541</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pmc">PMC4738359</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">26817514</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Grizzetti B, Poikane S. The importance of inland waters. In: Wetze