&#xe4;e K., J&#xf5;eleht A., and Plado J.. 2005. &#x201c;Cooling of the K&#xe4;rdla Impact Crater: 1. The Mineral Parasequence Observations.&#x201d; Meteoritics and Planetary Science 40: 3&#x2013;19.</Citation></Reference><Reference><Citation>Virtanen, P. , Gommers R., Oliphant T. E., et&#xa0;al. 2020. &#x201c;SciPy 1.0: Fundamental Algorithms for Scientific Computing in Python.&#x201d; Nature Methods 17: 261&#x2013;272.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pmc">PMC7056644</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">32015543</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Waskom, M. L. 2021. &#x201c;seaborn: statistical data visualization.&#x201d; Journal of Open Source Software 6: 3021.</Citation></Reference><Reference><Citation>Wolff&#x2010;Boenisch, D. , Gislason S. R., and Oelkers E. H.. 2006. &#x201c;The Effect of Crystallinity on Dissolution Rates and CO2 Consumption Capacity of Silicates.&#x201d; Geochimica et Cosmochimica Acta 70, no. 4: 858&#x2013;870.</Citation></Reference><Reference><Citation>Wu, Y.&#x2010;W. , Simmons B. A., and Singer S. W.. 2016. &#x201c;MaxBin 2.0: An Automated Binning Algorithm to Recover Genomes From Multiple Metagenomic Datasets.&#x201d; Bioinformatics 32: 605&#x2013;607.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">26515820</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference></ReferenceList></PubmedData></PubmedArticle><PubmedArticle><MedlineCitation Status="Publisher" Owner="NLM"><PMID Version="1">42887323</PMID><DateRevised><Year>2026</Year><Month>10</Month><Day>16</Day></DateRevised><Article PubModel="Print-Electronic"><Journal><ISSN IssnType="Electronic">1095-8630</ISSN><JournalIssue CitedMedium="Internet"><Volume>419</Volume><PubDate><Year>2026</Year><Month>Oct</Month><Day>16</Day></PubDate></JournalIssue><Title>Journal of environmental management</Title><ISOAbbreviation>J Environ Manage</ISOAbbreviation></Journal><ArticleTitle>Soil CH<sub>4</sub> and CO<sub>2</sub> emissions mediated by water and afforestation in Horqin Sandy Land.</ArticleTitle><Pagination><StartPage>131144</StartPage><MedlinePgn>131144</MedlinePgn></Pagination><ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.jenvman.2026.131144</ELocationID><ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0301-4797(26)02604-6</ELocationID><Abstract><AbstractText>Ecological restoration in arid and semi-arid regions plays a vital role in maintaining regional carbon balance under global change. Although water management and vegetation composition regulate soil carbon cycling, their combined effects on greenhouse gas source/sink dynamics in sandy ecosystems and the underlying microbial mechanisms remain unclear. This study examined sand dunes (Sd) and 35-year-old typical afforestation types including Caragana microphylla Lam. (Cm), Populus simonii Carr. (Ps), Pinus sylvestris var. mongolica Litv. (Pm), and mixed forests of Ps&#xa0;+&#xa0;Pm and Pm&#xa0;+&#xa0;Cm in the Horqin Sandy Land. Through a controlled incubation experiment at 30% and 60% water holding capacity (WHC), we systematically analyzed the effects of afforestation and water management on soil methane (CH<sub>4</sub>) and carbon dioxide (CO<sub>2</sub>) emissions. Linear regression analyses of functional gene abundances and environmental factors further revealed microbial mechanisms. All afforested soils acted as CH<sub>4</sub> sinks, Ps, Pm, Ps&#xa0;+&#xa0;Pm, and Pm&#xa0;+&#xa0;Cm showed the strongest CH<sub>4</sub> uptake, correlated with higher pmoA gene abundance and lower mcrA/pmoA. 30% WHC enhanced CH<sub>4</sub> oxidation by improving soil aeration, whereas CO<sub>2</sub> emissions increased significantly under elevated water content. Cm showed the highest CO<sub>2</sub> emissions, whereas mixed forests (Ps&#xa0;+&#xa0;Pm and Pm&#xa0;+&#xa0;Cm) maintained high CH<sub>4</sub> uptake even under high CO<sub>2</sub> emissions. These findings indicate that water management and afforestation legacy interactively shape carbon cycling pathways in sandy ecosystems. Mixed forests optimize microbial community function, balancing carbon cycling efficiency with greenhouse gas mitigation across water regimes, providing a scientific basis for carbon management in ecological restoration of the Horqin Sandy Land and comparable semi-arid regions.</AbstractText><CopyrightInformation>Copyright &#xa9; 2026 The Authors. Published by Elsevier Ltd.. All rights reserved.</CopyrightInformation></Abstract><AuthorList CompleteYN="Y"><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Fang</LastName><ForeName>Haifu</ForeName><Initials>H</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>State Key Laboratory of Ecological Safety and Sustainable Development in Arid Lands, National Field Observation and Research Station (Inner Mongolia Naiman) for Agro-Ecosystem, Northwest Institute of Eco-Environment and Resources, Chinese Academy of Sciences, Lanzhou, 730000, China; University of Chinese Academy of Sciences, Beijing, 100049, China.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Chen</LastName><ForeName>Xiang</ForeName><Initials>X</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>National Center of Pratacultural Technology Innovation (Under preparation), Hohhot, China.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Zhang</LastName><ForeName>Jian</ForeName><Initials>J</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>National Center of Pratacultural Technology Innovation (Under preparation), Hohhot, China.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Zhao</LastName><ForeName>Xiaolong</ForeName><Initials>X</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>College of Pastoral Agriculture Science and Technology, Lanzhou University, Lanzhou, 730000, China.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Li</LastName><ForeName>Yulin</ForeName><Initials>Y</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>State Key Laboratory of Ecological Safety and Sustainable Development in Arid Lands, National Field Observation and Research Station (Inner Mongolia Naiman) for Agro-Ecosystem, Northwest Institute of Eco-Environment and Resources, Chinese Academy of Sciences, Lanzhou, 730000, China. Electronic address: liyl@lzb.ac.cn.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author></AuthorList><Language>eng</Language><PublicationTypeList><PublicationType UI="D016428">Journal Article</PublicationType></PublicationTypeList><ArticleDate DateType="Electronic"><Year>2026</Year><Month>10</Month><Day>16</Day></ArticleDate></Article><MedlineJournalInfo><Country>England</Country><MedlineTA>J Environ Manage</MedlineTA><NlmUniqueID>0401664</NlmUniqueID><ISSNLinking>0301-4797</ISSNLinking></MedlineJournalInfo><CitationSubset>IM</CitationSubset><KeywordList Owner="NOTNLM"><Keyword MajorTopicYN="N">Afforestation</Keyword><Keyword MajorTopicYN="N">Carbon dioxide</Keyword><Keyword MajorTopicYN="N">Horqin Sandy Land</Keyword><Keyword MajorTopicYN="N">Methane</Keyword><Keyword MajorTopicYN="N">Water regime</Keyword></KeywordList><CoiStatement>Declaration of competing interest The authors declare no conflict of interest in this paper.</CoiStatement></MedlineCitation><PubmedData><History><PubMedPubDate PubStatus="received"><Year>2025</Year><Month>11</Month><Day>30</Day></PubMedPubDate><PubMedPubDate PubStatus="revised"><Year>2026</Year><Month>9</Month><Day>30</Day></PubMedPubDate><PubMedPubDate PubStatus="accepted"><Year>2026</Year><Month>10</Month><Day>10</Day></PubMedPubDate><PubMedPubDate PubStatus="medline"><Year>2026</Year><Month>10</Month><Day>16</Day><Hour>23</Hour><Minute>33</Minute></PubMedPubDate><PubMedPubDate PubStatus="pubmed"><Year>2026</Year><Month>10</Month><Day>16</Day><Hour>23</Hour><Minute>33</Minute></PubMedPubDate><PubMedPubDate PubStatus="entrez"><Year>2026</Year><Month>10</Month><Day>16</Day><Hour>18</Hour><Minute>12</Minute></PubMedPubDate></History><PublicationStatus>aheadofprint</PublicationStatus><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">42887323</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.1016/j.jenvman.2026.131144</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pii">S0301-4797(26)02604-6</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></PubmedData></PubmedArticle><PubmedArticle><MedlineCitation Status="PubMed-not-MEDLINE" Owner="NLM"><PMID Version="1">42852198</PMID><DateCompleted><Year>2026</Year><Month>10</Month><Day>09</Day></DateCompleted><DateRevised><Year>2026</Year><Month>10</Month><Day>10</Day></DateRevised><Article PubModel="Electronic-eCollection"><Journal><ISSN IssnType="Electronic">2470-1343</ISSN><JournalIssue CitedMedium="Internet"><Volume>11</Volume><Issue>39</Issue><PubDate><Year>2026</Year><Month>Oct</Month><Day>06</Day></PubDate></JournalIssue><Title>ACS omega</Title><ISOAbbreviation>ACS Omega</ISOAbbreviation></Journal><ArticleTitle>Microbial Dynamics during the Removal of Linear Alkylbenzenesulfonate (LAS) under Anaerobic and Microaerobic Conditions with Different Co-substrates.</ArticleTitle><Pagination><StartPage>58617</StartPage><EndPage>58633</EndPage><MedlinePgn>58617-58633</MedlinePgn></Pagination><ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1021/acsomega.6c01030</ELocationID><Abstract><AbstractText>Linear alkylbenzenesulfonate (LAS) is the most widely used anionic surfactant and is commonly found in wastewater due to its extensive use in detergents and cleaning products. Although LAS is biodegradable, its degradation in anaerobic environments remains a challenge, often requiring the addition of co-substrates and optimized operating conditions. This pioneering study evaluates the microbiological pathways and metabolisms underlying LAS biodegradation in UASB reactors operated under microaerated conditions, compared with strictly anaerobic systems. Furthermore, the effects of different co-substrates (ethanol and sucrose) on LAS removal efficiency and biogas production were also evaluated. The results indicate that microaeration, combined with sucrose as a co-substrate, significantly improves LAS removal, increasing efficiency by 34.3%. The study also revealed that microaeration influenced methane production, reducing its purity but increasing overall volumetric biogas production. Microaeration significantly influenced the microbial structure and function of the systems, depending on the co-substrate used. In the presence of ethanol, both systems showed a predominance of fermentative and methanogenic communities, with no significant differences in LAS removal. In contrast, with sucrose, microaeration promoted greater removal efficiency, associated with the selection of a specialized consortium composed of <i>Pleomorphomonas</i> and <i>Desulfovibrio</i>, capable of partially oxidizing the benzene ring and desulfonating the surfactant. This condition activated oxidative and sulfur metabolism pathways, while maintaining functional methanogenesis, resulting in a less diverse but metabolically more targeted and efficient community in LAS degradation. These findings highlight the potential of microaeration and co-substrate selection to optimize anaerobic treatment of LAS-containing wastewater while maintaining stable reactor performance.</AbstractText><CopyrightInformation>&#xa9; 2026 The Authors. Published by American Chemical Society.</CopyrightInformation></Abstract><AuthorList CompleteYN="Y"><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>de Oliveira</LastName><ForeName>Maur&#xed;cio Guimar&#xe3;es</ForeName><Initials>MG</Initials><Identifier Source="ORCID">0000-0003-2629-0301</Identifier><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Department of Hydraulic and Environmental Engineering, Federal University of Cear&#xe1;, 60455-900 Fortaleza, Cear&#xe1;, Brazil.</Affiliation><Identifier Source="RINGGOLD">28121</Identifier></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>da Silva</LastName><ForeName>Vicente El&#xed;cio Porfiro Sales Gon&#xe7;alves</ForeName><Initials>VEPSG</Initials><Identifier Source="ORCID">0000-0002-7269-6225</Identifier><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Department of Hydraulic and Environmental Engineering, Federal University of Cear&#xe1;, 60455-900 Fortaleza, Cear&#xe1;, Brazil.</Affiliation><Identifier Source="RINGGOLD">28121</Identifier></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>de Sousa E Silva</LastName><ForeName>Amanda</ForeName><Initials>A</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Department of Hydraulic and Environmental Engineering, Federal University of Cear&#xe1;, 60455-900 Fortaleza, Cear&#xe1;, Brazil.</Affiliation><Identifier Source="RINGGOLD">28121</Identifier></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Motteran</LastName><ForeName>Fabricio</ForeName><Initials>F</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Department of Civil Engineering, Centre for Technology and Geosciences, Federal University of Pernambuco, 50740-550 Recife, Brazil.</Affiliation><Identifier Source="RINGGOLD">28116</Identifier></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Dos Santos</LastName><ForeName>Andr&#xe9; Bezerra</ForeName><Initials>AB</Initials><Identifier Source="ORCID">0000-0002-3395-8878</Identifier><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Department of Hydraulic and Environmental Engineering, Federal University of Cear&#xe1;, 60455-900 Fortaleza, Cear&#xe1;, Brazil.</Affiliation><Identifier Source="RINGGOLD">28121</Identifier></AffiliationInfo></Author></AuthorList><Language>eng</Language><PublicationTypeList><PublicationType UI="D016428">Journal Article</PublicationType></PublicationTypeList><ArticleDate DateType="Electronic"><Year>2026</Year><Month>09</Month><Day>22</Day></ArticleDate></Article><MedlineJournalInfo><Country>United States</Country><MedlineTA>ACS Omega</MedlineTA><NlmUniqueID>101691658</NlmUniqueID><ISSNLinking>2470-1343</ISSNLinking></MedlineJournalInfo></MedlineCitation><PubmedData><History><PubMedPubDate PubStatus="received"><Year>2026</Year><Month>1</Month><Day>27</Day></PubMedPubDate><PubMedPubDate PubStatus="revised"><Year>2026</Year><Month>9</Month><Day>2</Day></PubMedPubDate><PubMedPubDate PubStatus="accepted"><Year>2026</Year><Month>9</Month><Day>4</Day></PubMedPubDate><PubMedPubDate PubStatus="medli