nce><Reference><Citation>A. Dobin, C. A. Davis, F. Schlesinger, et&#xa0;al., &#x201c;STAR: Ultrafast Universal RNA&#x2010;Seq Aligner,&#x201d; Bioinformatics 29, no. 1 (2013): 15&#x2013;21, https://doi.org/10.1093/bioinformatics/bts635.</Citation></Reference><Reference><Citation>R. Satija, J. A. Farrell, D. Gennert, A. F. Schier, and A. Regev, &#x201c;Spatial Reconstruction of Single&#x2010;Cell Gene Expression Data,&#x201d; Nature Biotechnology 33, no. 5 (2015): 495&#x2013;502, https://doi.org/10.1038/nbt.3192.</Citation></Reference><Reference><Citation>S. Choudhary and R. Satija, &#x201c;Comparison and Evaluation of Statistical Error Models for scRNA&#x2010;Seq,&#x201d; Genome Biology 23, no. 1 (2022): 1&#x2013;20, https://doi.org/10.1186/s13059&#x2010;021&#x2010;02584&#x2010;9.</Citation></Reference><Reference><Citation>H. Abdi and L. J. Williams, &#x201c;Principal Component Analysis,&#x201d; WIREs Comput Stat 2, no. 4 (2010): 433&#x2013;459, https://doi.org/10.1002/wics.101.</Citation></Reference><Reference><Citation>E. Becht, L. McInnes, J. Healy, et&#xa0;al., &#x201c;Dimensionality Reduction for Visualizing Single&#x2010;Cell Data Using UMAP,&#x201d; Nature Biotechnology 37, no. 1 (2019): 38&#x2013;44, https://doi.org/10.1038/nbt.4314.</Citation></Reference><Reference><Citation>A. Vandenbon and D. Diez, &#x201c;A Clustering&#x2010;independent Method for Finding Differentially Expressed Genes in Single&#x2010;Cell Transcriptome Data,&#x201d; Nature Communications 11, no. 1 (2020): 4318, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467&#x2010;020&#x2010;17900&#x2010;3.</Citation></Reference><Reference><Citation>S. Morabito, F. Reese, N. Rahimzadeh, E. Miyoshi, and V. Swarup, &#x201c;hdWGCNA Identifies Co&#x2010;Expression Networks in High&#x2010;Dimensional Transcriptomics Data,&#x201d; Cell Reports Methods 3, no. 6 (2023): 100498, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.crmeth.2023.100498.</Citation></Reference></ReferenceList></PubmedData></PubmedArticle><PubmedArticle><MedlineCitation Status="MEDLINE" Owner="NLM" IndexingMethod="Automated"><PMID Version="1">42833542</PMID><DateCompleted><Year>2026</Year><Month>10</Month><Day>22</Day></DateCompleted><DateRevised><Year>2026</Year><Month>10</Month><Day>22</Day></DateRevised><Article PubModel="Print-Electronic"><Journal><ISSN IssnType="Electronic">1096-0953</ISSN><JournalIssue CitedMedium="Internet"><Volume>309</Volume><Issue>Pt 2</Issue><PubDate><Year>2026</Year><Month>Nov</Month><Day>15</Day></PubDate></JournalIssue><Title>Environmental research</Title><ISOAbbreviation>Environ Res</ISOAbbreviation></Journal><ArticleTitle>Magnetite-assisted enhancement of stability and resilience in anaerobic digesters against hydraulic overloading and volatile fatty acid shocks.</ArticleTitle><Pagination><StartPage>125858</StartPage><MedlinePgn>125858</MedlinePgn></Pagination><ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.envres.2026.125858</ELocationID><ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0013-9351(26)02189-4</ELocationID><Abstract><AbstractText>Anaerobic digestion (AD) stability is often compromised under operational disturbances due to imbalances between acidogenesis and methanogenesis, where insufficient interspecies electron transfer (IET) can limit syntrophic oxidation of volatile fatty acids (VFAs). Conductive additives are widely recognized to enhance direct IET (DIET), yet the responses of electroactive microbial communities that support process resilience to operational disturbances remain unclear. Here, duplicate continuous AD reactors with and without submicron magnetite (Fe<sub>3</sub>O<sub>4</sub>) supplementation were operated under sequential VFA shocks and stepwise hydraulic retention time (HRT) reductions to examine process performance, sludge activity, and RNA-level microbial community dynamics. Following the second VFA shock of 4.5&#x202f;g chemical oxygen demand (COD)/L, magnetite-amended reactors stabilized within three days, whereas controls required up to ten days, reflecting improved syntrophic VFA oxidation, especially of propionate. Under stepwise HRT reduction, magnetite-amended reactors sustained a methane yield of 0.19&#x202f;L/g COD fed even at a 10-d HRT, whereas controls yielded only 0.01&#x202f;L/g COD fed with VFA accumulation. Magnetite also reduced toxic H<sub>2</sub>S accumulation with concurrent S<sup>0</sup> formation, suggesting an additional benefit for sulfide control. The improved performance coincided with higher electron transport and specific methanogenic activities, greater retention of putative electroactive microorganisms, and persistent dominance of Methanothrix. Potential bacterial partners of Methanothrix shifted with disturbance conditions, with Anaerolineaceae and Syntrophus retained during VFA shocks and Tolumonas, Syntrophus, and Geobacter becoming more prominent under short-HRT conditions. These findings suggest that magnetite contributes to AD resilience, potentially through electro-syntrophic interactions that support process stability during operational disturbances.</AbstractText><CopyrightInformation>Copyright &#xa9; 2026 Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.</CopyrightInformation></Abstract><AuthorList CompleteYN="Y"><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Choi</LastName><ForeName>Hyungmin</ForeName><Initials>H</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Department of Civil, Urban, Earth, and Environmental Engineering, Ulsan National Institute of Science and Technology (UNIST), Ulsan, 44919, Republic of Korea.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Kim</LastName><ForeName>Jinsu</ForeName><Initials>J</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Department of Civil, Urban, Earth, and Environmental Engineering, Ulsan National Institute of Science and Technology (UNIST), Ulsan, 44919, Republic of Korea.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Choi</LastName><ForeName>Yunjeong</ForeName><Initials>Y</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Department of Civil, Urban, Earth, and Environmental Engineering, Ulsan National Institute of Science and Technology (UNIST), Ulsan, 44919, Republic of Korea.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Park</LastName><ForeName>Jihun</ForeName><Initials>J</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Department of Civil, Urban, Earth, and Environmental Engineering, Ulsan National Institute of Science and Technology (UNIST), Ulsan, 44919, Republic of Korea.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Shin</LastName><ForeName>Woohyuk</ForeName><Initials>W</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Department of Civil, Urban, Earth, and Environmental Engineering, Ulsan National Institute of Science and Technology (UNIST), Ulsan, 44919, Republic of Korea.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Lee</LastName><ForeName>Changsoo</ForeName><Initials>C</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Department of Civil, Urban, Earth, and Environmental Engineering, Ulsan National Institute of Science and Technology (UNIST), Ulsan, 44919, Republic of Korea; Graduate School of Carbon Neutrality, Ulsan National Institute of Science and Technology (UNIST), Ulsan, 44919, Republic of Korea. Electronic address: cslee@unist.ac.kr.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author></AuthorList><Language>eng</Language><PublicationTypeList><PublicationType UI="D016428">Journal Article</PublicationType></PublicationTypeList><ArticleDate DateType="Electronic"><Year>2026</Year><Month>10</Month><Day>05</Day></ArticleDate></Article><MedlineJournalInfo><Country>Netherlands</Country><MedlineTA>Environ Res</MedlineTA><NlmUniqueID>0147621</NlmUniqueID><ISSNLinking>0013-9351</ISSNLinking></MedlineJournalInfo><ChemicalList><Chemical><RegistryNumber>0</RegistryNumber><NameOfSubstance UI="D005232">Fatty Acids, Volatile</NameOfSubstance></Chemical><Chemical><RegistryNumber>XM0M87F357</RegistryNumber><NameOfSubstance UI="D052203">Ferrosoferric Oxide</NameOfSubstance></Chemical><Chemical><RegistryNumber>0</RegistryNumber><NameOfSubstance UI="D012722">Sewage</NameOfSubstance></Chemical><Chemical><RegistryNumber>OP0UW79H66</RegistryNumber><NameOfSubstance UI="D008697">Methane</NameOfSubstance></Chemical></ChemicalList><CitationSubset>IM</CitationSubset><MeshHeadingList><MeshHeading><DescriptorName UI="D019149" MajorTopicYN="Y">Bioreactors</DescriptorName><QualifierName UI="Q000382" MajorTopicYN="N">microbiology</QualifierName></MeshHeading><MeshHeading><DescriptorName UI="D000693" MajorTopicYN="N">Anaerobiosis</DescriptorName></MeshHeading><MeshHeading><DescriptorName UI="D005232" MajorTopicYN="Y">Fatty Acids, Volatile</DescriptorName><QualifierName UI="Q000378" MajorTopicYN="N">metabolism</QualifierName></MeshHeading><MeshHeading><DescriptorName UI="D052203" MajorTopicYN="Y">Ferrosoferric Oxide</DescriptorName></MeshHeading><MeshHeading><DescriptorName UI="D012722" MajorTopicYN="N">Sewage</DescriptorName><QualifierName UI="Q000382" MajorTopicYN="N">microbiology</QualifierName></MeshHeading><MeshHeading><DescriptorName UI="D008697" MajorTopicYN="N">Methane</DescriptorName><QualifierName UI="Q000378" MajorTopicYN="N">metabolism</QualifierName></MeshHeading><MeshHeading><DescriptorName UI="D014865" MajorTopicYN="Y">Waste Disposal, Fluid</DescriptorName><QualifierName UI="Q000379" MajorTopicYN="N">methods</QualifierName></MeshHeading><MeshHeading><DescriptorName UI="D057919" MajorTopicYN="N">Biological Oxygen Demand Analysis</DescriptorName></MeshHeading></MeshHeadingList><KeywordList Owner="NOTNLM"><Keyword MajorTopicYN="N">Biogas</Keyword><Keyword MajorTopicYN="N">Electric syntrophy</Keyword><Keyword MajorTopicYN="N">Hydrogen sulfide oxidation</Keyword><Keyword MajorTopicYN="N">Operational disturbance</Keyword><Keyword MajorTopicYN="N">Process resilience</Keyword></KeywordList><CoiStatement>Declaration of competing interests The authors declare that they have no known competing financial interests or personal relationships that could have appeared to influence the work reported in this paper.</CoiStatement></MedlineCitation><PubmedData><History><PubMedPubDate PubStatus="received"><Year>2026</Year><Month>8</Month><Day>13</Day></PubMedPubDate><PubMedPubDate PubStatus="revised"><Year>2026</Year><Month>10</Month><Day>4</Day></PubMedPubDate><PubMedPubDate PubStatus="accepted"><Year>2026</Year><Month>10</Month><Day>4</Day></PubMedPubDate><PubMedPubDate PubStatus="medline"><Year>2026</Year><Month>10</Month><Day>22</Day><Hour>23</Hour><Minute>28</Minute></PubMedPubDate><PubMedPubDate PubStatus="pubmed"><Year>2026</Year><Month>10</Month><Day>5</Day><Hour>23</Hour><Minute>30</Minute></PubMedPubDate><PubMedPubDate PubStatus="entrez"><Year>2026</Year><Month>10</Month><Day>5</Day><Hour>19</Hour><Minute>35</Minute></PubMedPubDate></History><PublicationStatus>ppublish</PublicationStatus><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">42833542</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.1016/j.envres.2026.125858</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pii">S0013-9351(26)02189-4</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></PubmedData></PubmedArticle><PubmedArticle><MedlineCitation Status="PubMed-not-MEDLINE" Owner="NLM"><PMID Version="1">42827498</PMID><DateCompleted><Year>2026</Year><Month>10</Month><Day>03</Day></DateCompleted><DateRevised><Year>2026</Year><Month>10</Month><Day>05</Day></DateRevised><Article PubModel="Electronic-eCollection"><Journal><ISSN IssnType="Print">1664-302X</ISSN><JournalIssue CitedMedium="Print"><Volume>17</Volume><PubDate><Year>2026</Year></PubDate></JournalIssue><Title>Frontiers in microbiology</Title><ISOAbbreviation>Front Microbiol</ISOAbbreviation></Journal><ArticleTitle>Biochar mitigates dimethylarsenate-stimulated methane emissions mediated by DOM-microbe interactions in paddy soils.</ArticleTitle><Pagination><StartPage>1958612</StartPage><MedlinePgn>1958612</MedlinePgn></Pagination><ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">1958612</ELocationID><ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.3389/fmicb.2026.1958612</ELocationID><Abstract><AbstractText Label="INTRODUCTION" NlmCategory="UNASSIGNED">Paddy soils represent key biogeochemical interfaces linking arsenic (As) transformation and carbon (C) cycling. Dimethylarsenate (DMAs), a widespread organoarsenic contaminant in rice paddies, can alter methane (CH<sub>4</sub>) dynamics, but the underlying mechanisms based on dissolved organic matter (DOM)-microbe interaction and the mitigation potential of biochar still remained insufficiently understood.</AbstractText><AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="UNASSIGNED">Here, we investigated As biogeochemistry, CH<sub>4</sub> emissions, DOM quantity and composition, microbial community assembly and functional gene variations through a 98-day microcosm incubation experiment using paddy soil amended with DMAs alone and DMAs combined with biochar.</AbstractText><AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="UNASSIGNED">We found that DMAs greatly promoted CH&#x2084; emissions in a dose-dependent manner, with the DMAs120 treatment increasing cumulative CH&#x2084; emissions by 60.4% relative to the control. DMAs underwent significant biotransformation, declining from 98.7% on the 7th day to 26.2% of the total As by day 98. Porewater DOC concentrations were significantly increased by up to 1.81-fold (DMAs120, day 28). PARAFAC analysis revealed that DMAs shifted DOM composition toward labile protein-like fractions, with tryptophan-like (C2) and tyrosine-like (C3) components increasing while microbial humic-like (C1) and terrestrial humic-like (C4) fractions decreased. Such labile DOM precisely matched substrate requirements of enriched anaerobic fermentative microbes, establishing synergistic metabolic networks to supply methanogenic precursors. DMAs markedly increased <i>mcrA</i> gene abundance by 4.2-7.6 times with negligible change in <i>pmoA</i>, confirming stimulated methanogenesis rather than suppressed oxidation. By contrast, biochar effectively decreased % DMAs in porewater, reducing CH<sub>4</sub> emissions by 54.3% after 2% biochar amendment. Biochar also decreased DOC concentrations and protein-like DOM (C2, C3), reducing bioavailable substrates for methanogens. Furthermore, biochar suppressed <i>mcrA</i> gene abundances by 12.7-81.3%, while increased that of <i>pmoA</i> gene by 7.1-7.5 times, as well as increased porewater As(V) and enriched ammonia-oxidizing <i>Candidatus_Nitrosopelagicus</i> to potentially or indirectly stimulate anaerobic CH&#x2084; oxidation.</AbstractText><AbstractText Label="DISCUSSION" NlmCategory="UNASSIGNED">These results establish mechanistic connections between DMAs biotransformation, DOM turnover and CH<sub>4</sub> production in paddy soils, and verify that biochar restructures DOM-microbe interactions to mitigate DMAs-stimulated CH<sub>4</sub> emissions. This study deepens insights into As-C biogeochemical coupling and offers practical support for simultaneous As remediation and greenhouse gas control in paddy ecosystems.</AbstractText><CopyrightInformation>Copyright &#xa9; 2026 Kang, Jin, Du, Lv, Tang, Zhou, Liu, Yang and Jia.</CopyrightInformation></Abstract><AuthorList CompleteYN="Y"><Author ValidYN="Y" EqualContrib="Y"><LastName>Kang</LastName><ForeName>Yukun</ForeName><Initials>Y</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Key Laboratory of Urban Agriculture in North China, Ministry of Agriculture and Rural Affairs, Beijing University of Agriculture, Beijing, China.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>College of Bioscience and Resources Environment, Beijing University of Agriculture, Beijing, China.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y" EqualContrib="Y"><LastName>Jin</LastName><ForeName>Lei</ForeName><Initials>L</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Key Laboratory of Urban Agriculture in North China, Ministry of Agriculture and Rural Affairs, Beijing University of Agriculture, Beijing, China.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>College of Bioscience and Resources Environment, Beijing University of Agriculture, Beijing, China.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Du</LastName><ForeName>Jiyang</ForeName><Initials>J</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Key Laboratory of Urban Agriculture in North China, Ministry of Agriculture and Rural Affairs, Beijing University of Agriculture, Beijing, China.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>College of Bioscience and Resources Environment, Beijing University of Agriculture, Beijing, China.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Lv</LastName><ForeName>Jinpeng</ForeName><Initials>J</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Transport Planning and Research Institute, Ministry of Transport, Beijing, China.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Tang</LastName><ForeName>Yi</ForeName><Initials>Y</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Key Laboratory of Urban Agriculture in North China, Ministry of Agriculture and Rural Affairs, Beijing University of Agriculture, Beijing, China.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>College of Bioscience and Resources Environment, Beijing University of Agriculture, Beijing, China.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Zhou</LastName><ForeName>Jian</ForeName><Initials>J</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Key Laboratory of Urban Agriculture in North China, Ministry of Agriculture and Rural Affairs, Beijing University of Agriculture, Beijing, China.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>College of Bioscience and Resources Environment, Beijing University of Agriculture, Beijing, China.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Liu</LastName><ForeName>Jie</ForeName><Initials>J</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Key Laboratory of Urban Agriculture in North China, Ministry of Agriculture and Rural Affairs, Beijing University of Agriculture, Beijing, China.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>College of Bioscience and Resources Environment, Beijing University of Agriculture, Beijing, China.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Yang</LastName><ForeName>Yuping</ForeName><Initials>Y</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Key Laboratory of Urban Agriculture in North China, Ministry of Agriculture and Rural Affairs, Beijing University of Agriculture, Beijing, China.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>College of Bioscience and Resources Environment, Beijing University of Agriculture, Beijing, China.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Jia</LastName><ForeName>Yuehui</ForeName><Initials>Y</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Key Laboratory of Urban Agriculture in North China, Ministry of Agriculture and Rural Affairs, Beijing University of Agriculture, Beijing, China.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>College of Bioscience and Resources Environment, Beijing University of Agriculture, Beijing, China.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author></AuthorList><Language>eng</Language><PublicationTypeList><PublicationType UI="D016428">Journal Article</PublicationType></PublicationTypeList><ArticleDate DateType="Electronic"><Year>2026</Year><Month>09</Month><Day>18</Day></ArticleDate></Article><MedlineJournalInfo><Country>Switzerland</Country><MedlineTA>Front Microbiol</MedlineTA><NlmUniqueID>101548977</NlmUniqueID><ISSNLinking>1664-302X</ISSNLinking></MedlineJournalInfo><KeywordList Owner="NOTNLM"><Keyword MajorTopicYN="N">biochar</Keyword><Keyword MajorTopicYN="N">dissolved organic matter</Keyword><Keyword MajorTopicYN="N">methane</Keyword><Keyword MajorTopicYN="N">methylated arsenic</Keyword><Keyword MajorTopicYN="N">soil microorganisms</Keyword></KeywordList><CoiStatement>The author(s) declared that this work was conducted in the absence of any commercial or financial relationships that could be construed as a potential conflict of interest.</CoiStatement></MedlineCitation><PubmedData><History><PubMedPubDate PubStatus="received"><Year>2026</Year><Month>8</Month><Day>4</Day></PubMedPubDate><PubMedPubDate PubStatus="revised"><Year>2026</Year><Month>9</Month><Day>2</Day></PubMedPubDate><PubMedPubDate PubStatus="accepted"><Year>2026</Year><Month>9</Month><Day>8</Day></PubMedPubDate><PubMedPubDate PubStatus="medline"><Year>2026</Year><Month>10</Month><Day>4</Day><Hour>0</Hour><Minute>34</Minute></PubMedPubDate><PubMedPubDate PubStatus="pubmed"><Year>2026</Year><Month>10</Month><Day>4</Day><Hour>0</Hour><Minute>33</Minute></PubMedPubDate><PubMedPubDate PubStatus="entrez"><Year>2026</Year><Month>10</Month><Day>3</Day><Hour>5</Hour><Minute>7</Minute></PubMedPubDate><PubMedPubDate PubStatus="pmc-release"><Year>2026</Year><Month>9</Month><Day>18</Day></PubMedPubDate></History><PublicationStatus>epublish</PublicationStatus><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">42827498</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pmc">PMC13630724</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.3389/fmicb.2026.1958612</ArticleId></ArticleIdList><ReferenceList><Reference><Citation>An L., He S., Song X., Zheng Z., Li T., Han L. (2026). Long-term fertilization regimes mitigate dissolved organic matter leaching and alter its chemodiversity in agricultural soil leachate. Water Res. 297:125762. doi: 10.1016/j.watres.2026.125762,</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.1016/j.watres.2026.125762</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">41864007</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Bei Q. C., Peng J. J., Liesack W. (2021). Shedding light on the functional role of the Ignavibacteria in Italian rice field soil: a meta-genomic/transcriptomic analysis. Soil Biol. Biochem. 163:108444. doi: 10.1016/j.soilbio.2021.108444</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.1016/j.soilbio.2021.108444</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Br&