Boone DR, Castenholz RW, Garrity GM (ed), Bergey&#x2019;s manual of systematic bacteriology: volume one: the archaea and the deeply branching and phototrophic bacteria. Springer, New York, NY.</Citation></Reference><Reference><Citation>Fierer N, Lennon JT. 2011. The generation and maintenance of diversity in microbial communities. Am J Bot 98:439&#x2013;448. doi: 10.3732/ajb.1000498</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.3732/ajb.1000498</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">21613137</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Marsland R 3rd, Cui W, Goldford J, Sanchez A, Korolev K, Mehta P. 2019. Energy fluxes drive a transition in the diversity, stability, and functional structure of microbial communities. PLoS Comput Biol 15:e1006793. doi: 10.1371/journal.pcbi.1006793</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.1371/journal.pcbi.1006793</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pmc">PMC6386421</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">30721227</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Borges AV, Champenois W, Gypens N, Delille B, Harlay J. 2016. Massive marine methane emissions from near-shore shallow coastal areas. Sci Rep 6:27908. doi: 10.1038/srep27908</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.1038/srep27908</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pmc">PMC4901272</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">27283125</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>St. Pierre KA, Hunt BPV, Giesbrecht IJW, Tank SE, Lertzman KP, Del Bel Belluz J, Hessing-Lewis ML, Olson A, Froese T. 2022. Seasonally and spatially variable organic matter contributions from watershed, marine macrophyte, and pelagic sources to the northeast Pacific coastal ocean margin. Front Mar Sci 9:863209. doi: 10.3389/fmars.2022.863209</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.3389/fmars.2022.863209</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference></ReferenceList></PubmedData></PubmedArticle><PubmedArticle><MedlineCitation Status="PubMed-not-MEDLINE" Owner="NLM"><PMID Version="1">42798786</PMID><DateCompleted><Year>2026</Year><Month>09</Month><Day>26</Day></DateCompleted><DateRevised><Year>2026</Year><Month>09</Month><Day>28</Day></DateRevised><Article PubModel="Electronic-eCollection"><Journal><ISSN IssnType="Print">1664-302X</ISSN><JournalIssue CitedMedium="Print"><Volume>17</Volume><PubDate><Year>2026</Year></PubDate></JournalIssue><Title>Frontiers in microbiology</Title><ISOAbbreviation>Front Microbiol</ISOAbbreviation></Journal><ArticleTitle>Methanotrophic microbiomes for dilute coal-mine methane biofiltration: ecophysiological constraints and design priorities.</ArticleTitle><Pagination><StartPage>1953995</StartPage><MedlinePgn>1953995</MedlinePgn></Pagination><ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">1953995</ELocationID><ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.3389/fmicb.2026.1953995</ELocationID><Abstract><AbstractText>Dilute methane from coal-mine ventilation, drainage systems, and abandoned workings is difficult to treat because low methane partial pressure coincides with high or variable gas flow, short feasible residence times, fluctuating temperature and humidity, nutrient scarcity, and dust or trace contaminants. Aerobic methanotrophs can oxidize methane under moderate conditions, but mine-relevant performance cannot be inferred from isolate activity or gas-phase removal efficiency alone. This Mini Review organizes evidence by coal-mine source class and examines how methanotroph ecophysiology, partner organisms, carrier properties, hydrated biofilm structure, and gas transfer constrain sub-percent methane treatment. Mine-targeted studies comprise laboratory gas-phase reactors, mine-derived enrichments, batch experiments, and recent devices; no long-term validation under authentic mine-gas operation was identified in the targeted literature search. Transferable evidence comes from packed-bed reactors, landfill and compost systems, soils, and defined communities. These studies identify candidate traits that require mine-specific testing, including activity at the target methane partial pressure, recovery after starvation, nutrient economy, attachment, and stress tolerance. We propose source-matched functional microbiomes, activity-resolved methane balances, structured carriers, standardized reactor metrics, and explicit, site-specific no-go conditions as development priorities. Biofiltration should not advance where safe oxygen availability, practicable residence time, acceptable backpressure, or sustainable water and nutrient supply cannot be reconciled with mine operation.</AbstractText><CopyrightInformation>Copyright &#xa9; 2026 Wei and Ren.</CopyrightInformation></Abstract><AuthorList CompleteYN="Y"><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Wei</LastName><ForeName>Junjie</ForeName><Initials>J</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>School of Intelligent Engineering, Shandong Management University, Jinan, China.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Ren</LastName><ForeName>Haoyang</ForeName><Initials>H</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>School of Intelligent Engineering, Shandong Management University, Jinan, China.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author></AuthorList><Language>eng</Language><PublicationTypeList><PublicationType UI="D016428">Journal Article</PublicationType><PublicationType UI="D016454">Review</PublicationType></PublicationTypeList><ArticleDate DateType="Electronic"><Year>2026</Year><Month>09</Month><Day>11</Day></ArticleDate></Article><MedlineJournalInfo><Country>Switzerland</Country><MedlineTA>Front Microbiol</MedlineTA><NlmUniqueID>101548977</NlmUniqueID><ISSNLinking>1664-302X</ISSNLinking></MedlineJournalInfo><KeywordList Owner="NOTNLM"><Keyword MajorTopicYN="N">biofilm mass transfer</Keyword><Keyword MajorTopicYN="N">biofiltration</Keyword><Keyword MajorTopicYN="N">coal-mine methane</Keyword><Keyword MajorTopicYN="N">low-concentration methane</Keyword><Keyword MajorTopicYN="N">methanotrophic microbiome</Keyword><Keyword MajorTopicYN="N">microbial consortia</Keyword><Keyword MajorTopicYN="N">ventilation air methane</Keyword></KeywordList><CoiStatement>The author(s) declared that this work was conducted in the absence of any commercial or financial relationships that could be construed as a potential conflict of interest.</CoiStatement></MedlineCitation><PubmedData><History><PubMedPubDate PubStatus="received"><Year>2026</Year><Month>8</Month><Day>3</Day></PubMedPubDate><PubMedPubDate PubStatus="revised"><Year>2026</Year><Month>8</Month><Day>21</Day></PubMedPubDate><PubMedPubDate PubStatus="accepted"><Year>2026</Year><Month>9</Month><Day>3</Day></PubMedPubDate><PubMedPubDate PubStatus="medline"><Year>2026</Year><Month>9</Month><Day>27</Day><Hour>2</Hour><Minute>28</Minute></PubMedPubDate><PubMedPubDate PubStatus="pubmed"><Year>2026</Year><Month>9</Month><Day>27</Day><Hour>2</Hour><Minute>27</Minute></PubMedPubDate><PubMedPubDate PubStatus="entrez"><Year>2026</Year><Month>9</Month><Day>26</Day><Hour>5</Hour><Minute>41</Minute></PubMedPubDate><PubMedPubDate PubStatus="pmc-release"><Year>2026</Year><Month>9</Month><Day>11</Day></PubMedPubDate></History><PublicationStatus>epublish</PublicationStatus><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">42798786</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pmc">PMC13613084</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.3389/fmicb.2026.1953995</ArticleId></ArticleIdList><ReferenceList><Reference><Citation>Ahmadi F., Lackner M. (2024). Recent findings in methanotrophs: Genetics, molecular ecology, and biopotential.
