<i>Fuel</i>
349:128596. 10.1016/j.fuel.2023.128596</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.1016/j.fuel.2023.128596</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference></ReferenceList></PubmedData></PubmedArticle><PubmedArticle><MedlineCitation Status="MEDLINE" Owner="NLM" IndexingMethod="Automated"><PMID Version="1">42791248</PMID><DateCompleted><Year>2026</Year><Month>09</Month><Day>25</Day></DateCompleted><DateRevised><Year>2026</Year><Month>10</Month><Day>08</Day></DateRevised><Article PubModel="Electronic"><Journal><ISSN IssnType="Electronic">2041-1723</ISSN><JournalIssue CitedMedium="Internet"><Volume>17</Volume><Issue>1</Issue><PubDate><Year>2026</Year><Month>Sep</Month><Day>25</Day></PubDate></JournalIssue><Title>Nature communications</Title><ISOAbbreviation>Nat Commun</ISOAbbreviation></Journal><ArticleTitle>Active layer microbial inocula restore missing functions across thawed permafrost soils.</ArticleTitle><ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">10230</ELocationID><ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1038/s41467-026-78060-4</ELocationID><Abstract><AbstractText>Microbial dynamics in thawing permafrost induce a climate feedback of uncertain magnitude. Upon thaw, active layer microorganisms may establish in formerly frozen layers and introduce missing functions. Here we test the prevalence of functional limitations and how active layer microorganisms can mitigate these across four widespread permafrost types over 389 days. By restoring functions with a multifunctional microbial community, we demonstrate widespread functional limitations, as increased CO<sub>2</sub> emissions across four permafrost types, and onset N<sub>2</sub>O emissions in two of those. However, realistic active layer inocula increased CO<sub>2</sub> or N<sub>2</sub>O production less than the multifunctional community, due to unsuccessful coalescence or limitations in the inocula. Under anoxia, active layer inocula quintupled CH<sub>4</sub> while decreasing CO<sub>2</sub> production, resulting in a 35% increase in CO<sub>2</sub>-eq over six months. Understanding the functional limitations of permafrost microbial communities will be key to predicting their substantial, yet unaccounted, consequences on the biogeochemistry of the permafrost region.</AbstractText><CopyrightInformation>&#xa9; 2026. The Author(s).</CopyrightInformation></Abstract><AuthorList CompleteYN="Y"><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Monteux</LastName><ForeName>Sylvain</ForeName><Initials>S</Initials><Identifier Source="ORCID">0000-0001-9923-2036</Identifier><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Department of Soil and Environment, Swedish University of Agricultural Sciences, Uppsala, Sweden. sylvain.monteux@uit.no.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Department of Environmental Science, Stockholm University, Stockholm, Sweden. sylvain.monteux@uit.no.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Bolin Centre for Climate Research, Stockholm University, Stockholm, Sweden. sylvain.monteux@uit.no.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Troms&#xf8; Museum, UiT The Arctic University of Norway, Troms&#xf8;, Norway. sylvain.monteux@uit.no.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Dorrepaal</LastName><ForeName>Ellen</ForeName><Initials>E</Initials><Identifier Source="ORCID">0000-0002-0523-2471</Identifier><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Climate Impacts Research Centre, Ume&#xe5; University, Abisko, Sweden.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Fontaine</LastName><ForeName>S&#xe9;bastien</ForeName><Initials>S</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Grassland Ecosystem Research Unit, French National Institute for Food, Agriculture and Environment INRAE, Clermont-Ferrand, France.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Gavazov</LastName><ForeName>Konstantin</ForeName><Initials>K</Initials><Identifier Source="ORCID">0000-0003-4479-7202</Identifier><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Swiss Federal Institute for Forest, Snow and Landscape Research WSL, Birmensdorf, Switzerland.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Hallin</LastName><ForeName>Sara</ForeName><Initials>S</Initials><Identifier Source="ORCID">0000-0002-9069-9024</Identifier><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Department of Forest Mycology and Plant Pathology, Swedish University of Agricultural Sciences, Uppsala, Sweden.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Science for Life Laboratory, Swedish University of Agricultural Sciences, Uppsala, Sweden.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Juhanson</LastName><ForeName>Jaanis</ForeName><Initials>J</Initials><Identifier Source="ORCID">0000-0003-3799-2819</Identifier><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Department of Forest Mycology and Plant Pathology, Swedish University of Agricultural Sciences, Uppsala, Sweden.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Keuper</LastName><ForeName>Frida</ForeName><Initials>F</Initials><Identifier Source="ORCID">0000-0001-8673-7991</Identifier><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>BioEcoAgro Joint Cross-Border Research Unit, French National Institute for Food, Agriculture and Environment INRAE, Laon, France.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Tveit</LastName><ForeName>Alexander T</ForeName><Initials>AT</Initials><Identifier Source="ORCID">0000-0002-4163-3416</Identifier><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Department of Arctic and Marine Biology, UiT The Arctic University of Norway, Troms&#xf8;, Norway.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Walz</LastName><ForeName>Josefine</ForeName><Initials>J</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Climate Impacts Research Centre, Ume&#xe5; University, Abisko, Sweden.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Wegner</LastName><ForeName>Rica</ForeName><Initials>R</Initials><AffiliationIn