ting. J. Environ. Qual. 2011;40:199&#x2013;205. doi: 10.2134/jeq2010.0088.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.2134/jeq2010.0088</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">21488508</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Thauer R.K., Kaster A.-K., Seedorf H., Buckel W., Hedderich R. Methanogenic archaea: Ecologically relevant differences in energy conservation. Nat. Rev. Microbiol. 2008;6:579&#x2013;591. doi: 10.1038/nrmicro1931.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.1038/nrmicro1931</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">18587410</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Hua Z.-S., Wang Y.-L., Evans P.N., Qu Y.-N., Goh K.M., Rao Y.-Z., Qi Y.-L., Li Y.-X., Huang M.-J., Jiao J.-Y., et al. Insights into the ecological roles and evolution of methyl-coenzyme M reductase-containing hot spring Archaea. Nat. Commun. 2019;10:4574. doi: 10.1038/s41467-019-12574-y.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.1038/s41467-019-12574-y</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pmc">PMC6783470</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">31594929</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Timmers P.H.A., Welte C.U., Koehorst J.J., Plugge C.M., Jetten M.S.M., Stams A.J.M. Reverse Methanogenesis and Respiration in Methanotrophic Archaea. Archaea. 2017;2017:1654237. doi: 10.1155/2017/1654237.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.1155/2017/1654237</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pmc">PMC5244752</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">28154498</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Chen H., Gan Q., Fan C. Methyl-Coenzyme M Reductase and Its Post-translational Modifications. Front. Microbiol. 2020;11:578356. doi: 10.3389/fmicb.2020.578356.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.3389/fmicb.2020.578356</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pmc">PMC7581889</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">33162960</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Cai Y., Zheng Y., Bodelier P.L.E., Conrad R., Jia Z. Conventional methanotrophs are responsible for atmospheric methane oxidation in paddy soils. Nat. Commun. 2016;7:11728. doi: 10.1038/ncomms11728.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.1038/ncomms11728</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pmc">PMC4895445</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">27248847</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Lawton T.J., Rosenzweig A.C. Methane-Oxidizing Enzymes: An Upstream Problem in Biological Gas-to-Liquids Conversion. J. Am. Chem. Soc. 2016;138:9327&#x2013;9340. doi: 10.1021/jacs.6b04568.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.1021/jacs.6b04568</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pmc">PMC5242187</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">27366961</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Qi Z.-w., Wang L., Guan J., Zhang B., Wang L., Feng C.-c., Wang D., Zhang Y. Phycosphere microbial assembly reinforces microalgae-driven soil organic carbon sequestration and methane mitigation in black soil paddies. J. Clean. Prod. 2026;560:148208. doi: 10.1016/j.jclepro.2026.148208.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.1016/j.jclepro.2026.148208</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Narengerile A., Deng A., Dong W., Raheem A., Li J., Wang S., Shang Z., Chen Z., Song H., Zhang J. Integrated effects of aerobic tillage and controlled irrigation on rice yield and greenhouse gas emissions in a high-latitude rice growing region. Crop J. 2026 doi: 10.1016/j.cj.2026.03.012. in press .</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.1016/j.cj.2026.03.012</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference></ReferenceList></PubmedData></PubmedArticle><PubmedArticle><MedlineCitation Status="MEDLINE" Owner="NLM" IndexingMethod="Automated"><PMID Version="1">42269882</PMID><DateCompleted><Year>2026</Year><Month>07</Month><Day>21</Day></DateCompleted><DateRevised><Year>2026</Year><Month>07</Month><Day>21</Day></DateRevised><Article PubModel="Print-Electronic"><Journal><ISSN IssnType="Electronic">1873-2976</ISSN><JournalIssue CitedMedium="Internet"><Volume>458</Volume><PubDate><Year>2026</Year><Month>Oct</Month></PubDate></JournalIssue><Title>Bioresource technology</Title><ISOAbbreviation>Bioresour Technol</ISOAbbreviation></Journal><ArticleTitle>Enhanced biomethane production associated with olivine-stimulated CO<sub>2</sub> reduction in petroleum reservoir production water.</ArticleTitle><Pagination><StartPage>135131</StartPage><MedlinePgn>135131</MedlinePgn></Pagination><ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.biortech.2026.135131</ELocationID><ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0960-8524(26)01213-7</ELocationID><Abstract><AbstractText>Biomethanation, in which microbes convert CO<sub>2</sub> into biomethane (bCH<sub>4</sub>), is a promising strategy for simultaneously recovering bioenergy and managing carbon. Electron donors are critical regulators of microbial CO<sub>2</sub> reduction. However, a knowledge gap exists regarding both olivine as an electron donor in petroleum production water and its comparison with other mineral-based electron donors. Therefore, this study investigated the potential of natural olivine and iron ore to stimulate bCH<sub>4</sub> production under laboratory-simulated petroleum reservoir conditions. Natural olivine-amended microcosms exhibited a high bCH<sub>4</sub> production of 218.59&#xa0;&#x3bc;mol/L&#xb7;g of olivine, indicating a 49.04% increase compared to those amended with natural iron ore. The results also suggested that acetate and residual organic carbon present in the production water likely served as additional substrates for bCH<sub>4</sub> production. Additionally, 16S rRNA gene sequencing revealed that the microbial communities responsible for bCH<sub>4</sub> production were dominated by CO<sub>2</sub>-reducing methanogens closely related to Methanothermobacter and Methanobacterium. To the best of our knowledge, this study is the first attempt to unravel the use of natural olivine in this context, offering a novel strategy for CO<sub>2</sub> reduction and bCH<sub>4</sub> energy production.</AbstractText><CopyrightInformation>Copyright &#xa9; 2026 Elsevier Ltd. All rights reserved.</CopyrightInformation></Abstract><AuthorList CompleteYN="Y"><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Javed</LastName><ForeName>Muhammad</ForeName><Initials>M</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>State Key Laboratory of Bioreactor Engineering, East China University of Science and Technology, Shanghai 200237, PR China.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Irfan</LastName><ForeName>Muhammad</ForeName><Initials>M</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Department of Chemical, Polymer and Composite Materials Engineering, University of Engineering &amp; Technology, New Campus, Lahore 2902, Pakistan.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Chen</LastName><ForeName>Le-Gang</ForeName><Initials>LG</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>State Key Laboratory of Bioreactor Engineering, East China University of Science and Technology, Shanghai 200237, PR China.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Fang</LastName><ForeName>Bo</ForeName><Initials>B</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>State Key Laboratory of Bioreactor Engineering, East China University of Science and Technology, Shanghai 200237, PR China.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Wang</LastName><ForeName>Bo-Wen</ForeName><Initials>BW</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>State Key Laboratory of Bioreactor Engineering, East China University of Science and Technology, Shanghai 200237, PR China.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Dastgir</LastName><ForeName>Ghulam</ForeName><Initials>G</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>State Key Laboratory of Bioreactor Engineering, East China University of Science and Technology, Shanghai 200237, PR Chi